==English==

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|frm|imprudent}}, from {{der|en|la|imprūdens|t=not foreseeing, ignorant}}, prefix {{m|la|in-|im-|t=[[not]]}} + {{m|la|prūdens|t=foreseeing, skilled, judicious}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɪmˈpɹudənt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-imprudent.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Not [[prudent]]; lacking [[prudence]] or [[discretion]]; [[indiscreet]]; [[injudicious]]; not paying attention to the [[consequence]]s of one's actions
#: {{syn|en|indiscreet|injudicious|incautious|ill-advised|unwise|heedless|careless|rash|negligent}}
#: {{ant|en|prudent}}
#* {{RQ:Strype MP}}
#*: Here Her Majesty took a great dislike at the '''imprudent''' behavior of many of the Ministers and Readers.
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1853
|author=Mary Elizabeth Braddon
|title=Phantom Fortune
|chapter=3
|url=http://ebooks.adelaide.edu.au/v/verne/jules/v52jr/chapter3.html
|passage=‘It was a most ''''imprudent''' thing to go up Helvellyn in such weather,’ said Fräulein Müller, shaking her head gloomily as she ate her fish.}}
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1864
|author=Jules Verne
|title=Journey to the Interior of the Earth
|chapter=3
|url=http://ebooks.adelaide.edu.au/v/verne/jules/v52jr/chapter3.html
|passage=My uncle, falling back into his absorbing contemplations, had already forgotten my '''imprudent''' words. I merely say '''imprudent''', for the great mind of so learned a man of course had no place for love affairs, and happily the grand business of the document gained me the victory.}}

